abstract: >
  When a plot of land is converted into a token, a court order that prevents
  the sale of the property must be made into an action on the blockchain. Token
  standards for regulated tokens (such as ERC-3643) currently allow a token to
  be frozen; however, these standards do not address who will decide to trigger
  this freeze. Currently, this decision is left up to a single administrative
  "key," so whoever possesses (or misuses or steals) this "key" can arbitrarily
  freeze or unfreeze land regardless of whether a valid court order exists. To
  provide additional security against unauthorized freezing/unfreezing of land
  by administrators, we are presenting CourtKeyRegistry, a smart contract that
  acts as a proxy to the token's freeze function and will only accept a freeze
  if it verifies the existence of a valid court order on the blockchain. Instead
  of relying upon the caller (the administrator), CourtKeyRegistry rebuilds the
  ordered digital signature and executes 12 checks before allowing a freeze and
  13 checks before releasing. The CourtKeyRegistry is the only entity allowed
  to freeze the token. Once an acceptable order is received, CourtKeyRegistry
  creates a permanent, publicly accessible record that includes information
  about who signed the order and what checks were performed during verification.
  We have developed a functional prototype of CourtKeyRegistry called
  NyayaChain. We have tested NyayaChain using a Hyperledger Besu blockchain
  network with four validators running under QBFT consensus. During testing we
  successfully ran all 43 cases in our test suite on both a local Hardhat
  environment and our deployed Besu environment. We also measured the average
  time it took for 100 three-signature freeze orders to receive final
  confirmation in our deployed Besu environment. These measurements showed that
  the average confirmation time was 1994 milliseconds and occurred well within
  our defined 2000ms block period.
